1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:03,710
In video one, we saw that data becomes vectors.

2
00:00:04,122 --> 00:00:07,327
But a model almost never looks at just one vector.

3
00:00:07,752 --> 00:00:09,906
It looks at a whole table of them at once.

4
00:00:10,345 --> 00:00:14,720
And every layer of a neural network keeps what it has learned in a grid of numbers.

5
00:00:15,146 --> 00:00:16,409
That grid has a name.

6
00:00:16,835 --> 00:00:18,018
It is called a matrix.

7
00:00:18,939 --> 00:00:22,756
In this video you will learn the two faces of a matrix.

8
00:00:23,181 --> 00:00:28,115
It is a table that stores data, and it is a machine that moves vectors.

9
00:00:28,554 --> 00:00:33,899
You will multiply a matrix by a vector, and two matrices together, by hand.

10
00:00:34,338 --> 00:00:40,256
You only need video one: vectors, adding, scaling, and linear combinations.

11
00:00:41,973 --> 00:00:44,579
Let's start with the first face: a table.

12
00:00:45,018 --> 00:00:46,374
Here are three houses.

13
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:54,353
For each house we write three numbers: the area in square meters, the number of bedrooms, and the age in years.

14
00:00:54,792 --> 00:00:57,571
Each house is a vector, just like in video one.

15
00:00:57,996 --> 00:01:00,989
Now we stack the three vectors, one per row.

16
00:01:01,906 --> 00:01:05,244
The result is a grid of numbers with rows and columns.

17
00:01:05,669 --> 00:01:08,728
A rectangular grid of numbers is called a matrix.

18
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:15,204
This one has three rows and three columns, so we call it a three by three matrix.

19
00:01:15,643 --> 00:01:19,712
We always say the number of rows first, and then the number of columns.

20
00:01:20,639 --> 00:01:23,046
Each number in the matrix has an address.

21
00:01:23,485 --> 00:01:28,658
We write the letter a with two small numbers: first the row, then the column.

22
00:01:29,070 --> 00:01:32,727
So a two one is the number in row two, column one.

23
00:01:33,166 --> 00:01:36,224
Here that is eighty, the area of the second house.

24
00:01:37,126 --> 00:01:39,520
Read the matrix in two directions.

25
00:01:39,932 --> 00:01:42,379
A row is one house: one data point.

26
00:01:42,791 --> 00:01:46,195
A column is one feature across all the houses.

27
00:01:46,687 --> 00:01:52,139
For example, the second column lists the bedrooms: three, two, and four.

28
00:01:52,551 --> 00:01:59,041
In machine learning, a batch of data is stored exactly like this, with one row per example.

29
00:02:00,759 --> 00:02:03,086
Now the second face: a machine.

30
00:02:03,499 --> 00:02:07,408
A matrix can take a vector as input and give back a new vector.

31
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:10,028
Here is a two by two matrix A.

32
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:15,680
Its first column is two, one, and its second column is one, three.

33
00:02:16,132 --> 00:02:19,961
Here is an input vector x, equal to one, two.

34
00:02:20,387 --> 00:02:21,491
What is A times x?

35
00:02:22,419 --> 00:02:24,015
Here is the key idea.

36
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:28,084
A times x is a linear combination of the columns of A.

37
00:02:28,523 --> 00:02:30,637
The components of x are the weights.

38
00:02:31,076 --> 00:02:35,823
So we take one times the first column, plus two times the second column.

39
00:02:36,249 --> 00:02:38,297
One times two, one is two, one.

40
00:02:38,736 --> 00:02:41,462
Two times one, three is two, six.

41
00:02:42,406 --> 00:02:43,297
Now add them.

42
00:02:43,736 --> 00:02:45,398
Two plus two is four.

43
00:02:45,837 --> 00:02:47,645
One plus six is seven.

44
00:02:48,071 --> 00:02:51,023
So A times x equals four, seven.

45
00:02:51,462 --> 00:02:55,983
The machine turned the vector one, two into the vector four, seven.

46
00:02:56,409 --> 00:03:01,887
Every matrix times a vector works this way, no matter how big the matrix is.

47
00:03:02,852 --> 00:03:05,924
There is a second way to get the same answer: the row view.

48
00:03:06,350 --> 00:03:10,592
Take the first row of A, two, one, and the vector one, two.

49
00:03:11,017 --> 00:03:13,491
Multiply matching entries and add.

50
00:03:13,916 --> 00:03:16,802
Two times one plus one times two is four.

51
00:03:17,241 --> 00:03:22,520
The second row gives one times one plus three times two, which is seven.

52
00:03:23,473 --> 00:03:25,361
Same answer: four, seven.

53
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:33,007
This multiply-and-add pattern between a row and a vector is so important that it has its own name, the inner product.

54
00:03:33,446 --> 00:03:35,574
It gets its own video, video five.

55
00:03:35,999 --> 00:03:42,103
One rule about shapes: the matrix needs as many columns as the vector has components.

56
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:46,326
Let's watch the machine at work on the whole plane.

57
00:03:46,778 --> 00:03:49,491
Here is a grid with two special arrows.

58
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:53,081
The blue arrow, e one, is one, zero.

59
00:03:53,507 --> 00:03:56,486
The green arrow, e two, is zero, one.

60
00:03:56,911 --> 00:03:59,704
We also follow a yellow vector, one, two.

61
00:04:00,142 --> 00:04:01,605
Now look at the column view again.

62
00:04:02,044 --> 00:04:04,052
A times e one is just the first column.

63
00:04:04,478 --> 00:04:06,592
A times e two is the second column.

64
00:04:07,539 --> 00:04:12,353
So the columns tell you everything: they say where e one and e two land.

65
00:04:12,792 --> 00:04:16,861
Take the matrix with columns two, zero and zero, one.

66
00:04:17,273 --> 00:04:22,034
It sends e one to two, zero, and it leaves e two where it is.

67
00:04:22,459 --> 00:04:23,138
Watch the grid.

68
00:04:23,563 --> 00:04:27,566
Everything becomes twice as wide, and the heights stay the same.

69
00:04:28,493 --> 00:04:31,870
The yellow vector one, two became two, two.

70
00:04:32,309 --> 00:04:33,612
Check it with the column rule.

71
00:04:34,051 --> 00:04:38,439
One times two, zero, plus two times zero, one, is two, two.

72
00:04:38,878 --> 00:04:40,886
The picture and the arithmetic agree.

73
00:04:41,826 --> 00:04:47,025
Now a different matrix, with columns zero, one and minus one, zero.

74
00:04:47,464 --> 00:04:52,677
It sends e one to zero, one, and e two to minus one, zero.

75
00:04:53,102 --> 00:04:55,882
Each arrow turns a quarter turn to the left.

76
00:04:56,307 --> 00:05:01,720
So this matrix rotates the whole plane by ninety degrees, counterclockwise.

77
00:05:02,639 --> 00:05:06,323
The yellow vector one, two now becomes minus two, one.

78
00:05:06,748 --> 00:05:07,892
Notice what stays true.

79
00:05:08,331 --> 00:05:15,153
The grid lines stay straight, parallel, and evenly spaced, and the origin does not move.

80
00:05:15,565 --> 00:05:18,557
Machines with this property are called linear maps.

81
00:05:18,982 --> 00:05:21,535
They get their own video, video four.

82
00:05:23,273 --> 00:05:26,411
What happens if we use two machines, one after the other?

83
00:05:26,890 --> 00:05:28,525
First apply B to x.

84
00:05:28,964 --> 00:05:30,826
Then apply A to the result.

85
00:05:31,265 --> 00:05:34,695
Is there one single matrix that does both steps at once?

86
00:05:35,108 --> 00:05:35,600
Yes.

87
00:05:36,025 --> 00:05:37,794
It is the product, A B.

88
00:05:38,379 --> 00:05:42,089
Note the order: B acts first, because B stands next to x.

89
00:05:43,593 --> 00:05:47,103
Computing this product is called matrix multiplication.

90
00:05:47,542 --> 00:05:54,297
To find the entry in row i and column j of A B, take row i of A and column j of B.

91
00:05:54,949 --> 00:05:57,648
Multiply matching entries and add them up.

92
00:05:58,074 --> 00:06:05,295
In symbols: A B, i j, equals the sum over k of a i k times b k j.

93
00:06:06,793 --> 00:06:08,016
Let's do one completely.

94
00:06:08,428 --> 00:06:12,125
A has rows one, two and three, four.

95
00:06:12,564 --> 00:06:15,250
B has rows five, six and seven, eight.

96
00:06:15,689 --> 00:06:23,215
Top left: row one of A, which is one, two, with column one of B, which is five, seven.

97
00:06:23,654 --> 00:06:26,753
One times five plus two times seven is nineteen.

98
00:06:27,178 --> 00:06:31,500
Top right: one times six plus two times eight is twenty-two.

99
00:06:32,452 --> 00:06:38,676
Bottom left: row two of A, three, four, with column one of B, five, seven.

100
00:06:39,141 --> 00:06:42,546
Three times five plus four times seven is forty-three.

101
00:06:42,971 --> 00:06:47,266
Bottom right: three times six plus four times eight is fifty.

102
00:06:47,705 --> 00:06:52,359
So A B has rows nineteen, twenty-two and forty-three, fifty.

103
00:06:53,873 --> 00:06:54,737
Now the shape rule.

104
00:06:55,162 --> 00:07:01,625
If A is m by n, and B is n by p, then A B is m by p.

105
00:07:02,064 --> 00:07:05,123
The two inner numbers must match, and they disappear.

106
00:07:05,548 --> 00:07:10,748
A two by three matrix times a three by two matrix gives a two by two matrix.

107
00:07:11,173 --> 00:07:14,777
But a two by three times a two by three is not allowed.

108
00:07:16,332 --> 00:07:18,819
Here is a surprise: the order matters.

109
00:07:19,245 --> 00:07:21,519
Take the stretch S and the rotation R.

110
00:07:22,090 --> 00:07:28,686
Stretch first, then rotate: e one goes to two, zero, and then to zero, two.

111
00:07:29,098 --> 00:07:36,612
Rotate first, then stretch: e one goes to zero, one, and the stretch leaves it at zero, one.

112
00:07:37,037 --> 00:07:38,194
The results are different.

113
00:07:39,106 --> 00:07:41,220
The matrices agree with the pictures.

114
00:07:41,646 --> 00:07:43,867
R S has the first column zero, two.

115
00:07:44,292 --> 00:07:46,765
S R has the first column zero, one.

116
00:07:47,191 --> 00:07:49,531
So R S is not equal to S R.

117
00:07:49,957 --> 00:07:53,640
With numbers, a times b always equals b times a.

118
00:07:54,079 --> 00:07:58,242
With matrices, in general, A B is not equal to B A.

119
00:07:59,973 --> 00:08:02,366
Two more tools finish the toolbox.

120
00:08:02,792 --> 00:08:04,534
The first is the transpose.

121
00:08:04,959 --> 00:08:10,172
Take a matrix and flip it over its diagonal, so its rows become its columns.

122
00:08:10,611 --> 00:08:14,135
We write a small T at the top right, and we say A transpose.

123
00:08:15,086 --> 00:08:21,695
Here is a two by three matrix, with rows one, two, three and four, five, six.

124
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:24,381
Its transpose is three by two.

125
00:08:24,807 --> 00:08:29,009
The first row, one, two, three, becomes the first column.

126
00:08:29,434 --> 00:08:31,642
The second row becomes the second column.

127
00:08:32,067 --> 00:08:35,325
The shape flips: m by n becomes n by m.

128
00:08:36,239 --> 00:08:37,822
Careful with a common mistake.

129
00:08:38,247 --> 00:08:44,378
The transpose is not the same as pouring the numbers, in reading order, into a new shape.

130
00:08:44,816 --> 00:08:48,500
That gives one, two, then three, four, then five, six: wrong.

131
00:08:48,925 --> 00:08:55,189
In the transpose, the entry in row i, column j comes from row j, column i.

132
00:08:56,752 --> 00:08:59,399
The second tool is the identity matrix.

133
00:08:59,838 --> 00:09:03,229
It has ones on the diagonal and zeros everywhere else.

134
00:09:03,627 --> 00:09:09,598
Its columns are e one and e two, so it leaves e one and e two where they are.

135
00:09:10,037 --> 00:09:12,604
So the identity matrix does nothing.

136
00:09:13,029 --> 00:09:16,167
I times x equals x, for every x.

137
00:09:17,106 --> 00:09:21,707
It is the matrix version of the number one, because one times five equals five.

138
00:09:22,146 --> 00:09:31,694
Multiply any matrix by the identity, on either side, and you get it back: A times I equals A, and I times A equals A.

139
00:09:33,239 --> 00:09:34,542
Where does all of this come from?

140
00:09:34,955 --> 00:09:38,798
A matrix times a vector is a linear combination of its columns.

141
00:09:39,223 --> 00:09:43,492
Matrix multiplication is one such machine after another.

142
00:09:43,931 --> 00:09:47,734
So everything in this video is built from adding and scaling.

143
00:09:48,173 --> 00:09:50,553
That is axiom A, linearity.

144
00:09:52,273 --> 00:09:54,320
Now let's put matrices to work.

145
00:09:54,759 --> 00:09:59,174
Take our three houses again, as a three by three matrix X.

146
00:09:59,467 --> 00:10:05,570
Suppose a toy model gives each house a score with three weights: two, ten, and minus one.

147
00:10:06,009 --> 00:10:08,097
We put the weights into a vector w.

148
00:10:08,536 --> 00:10:12,432
Then X times w scores all three houses at once.

149
00:10:13,339 --> 00:10:18,632
Row one: two times one hundred twenty, plus ten times three, minus fifteen.

150
00:10:19,057 --> 00:10:24,164
That is two hundred forty plus thirty minus fifteen, which is two hundred fifty-five.

151
00:10:24,602 --> 00:10:29,004
The other rows give one hundred fifty and three hundred thirty-five.

152
00:10:29,430 --> 00:10:32,222
One matrix product gives three predictions.

153
00:10:32,634 --> 00:10:35,321
This is why data is kept in matrices.

154
00:10:36,873 --> 00:10:39,612
A layer in a neural network works the same way.

155
00:10:40,024 --> 00:10:46,115
A layer with three inputs and two outputs has a two by three matrix of weights, W.

156
00:10:46,553 --> 00:10:50,623
The layer multiplies the input vector x by this matrix.

157
00:10:51,048 --> 00:10:54,718
Each output is one row of weights combined with the input.

158
00:10:56,239 --> 00:10:57,875
Real models are much bigger.

159
00:10:58,274 --> 00:11:07,210
In the book Build a Large Language Model from Scratch, one example layer has a weight matrix of two thousand forty-eight by two thousand forty-eight.

160
00:11:07,635 --> 00:11:10,960
That is more than four million numbers in a single matrix.

161
00:11:12,673 --> 00:11:13,510
Now it is your turn.

162
00:11:13,962 --> 00:11:15,731
Pause the video and try this.

163
00:11:16,143 --> 00:11:22,925
Multiply the matrix with rows one, two and zero, one by the vector three, one.

164
00:11:23,351 --> 00:11:25,252
Use the column view or the row view.

165
00:11:25,691 --> 00:11:26,954
Both give the same answer.

166
00:11:27,886 --> 00:11:29,814
Here is the answer with the row view.

167
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:34,495
Row one: one times three plus two times one is five.

168
00:11:34,934 --> 00:11:39,189
Row two: zero times three plus one times one is one.

169
00:11:39,615 --> 00:11:41,529
So the answer is five, one.

170
00:11:41,955 --> 00:11:48,896
With the column view, three times one, zero plus one times two, one also gives five, one.

171
00:11:50,439 --> 00:11:52,527
Second question, about shapes.

172
00:11:52,952 --> 00:11:57,620
A is a two by three matrix, and B is a three by four matrix.

173
00:11:58,059 --> 00:12:00,120
Is A B allowed, and what is its shape?

174
00:12:00,546 --> 00:12:01,476
Is B A allowed?

175
00:12:01,915 --> 00:12:03,617
Pause and use the shape rule.

176
00:12:04,539 --> 00:12:08,914
A B is allowed, because the inner numbers are three and three.

177
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:11,175
The result is two by four.

178
00:12:11,614 --> 00:12:12,704
B A is not allowed.

179
00:12:13,130 --> 00:12:18,529
B is three by four and A is two by three, so the inner numbers are four and two.

180
00:12:18,954 --> 00:12:19,938
They do not match.

181
00:12:20,364 --> 00:12:22,185
Even the shape can depend on the order.

182
00:12:23,906 --> 00:12:29,424
Let's check everything in Python with NumPy, where the at sign multiplies matrices.

183
00:12:29,850 --> 00:12:37,802
We compute A times x, the stretch and the rotation, both orders of the product, the transpose, and the identity check.

184
00:12:39,306 --> 00:12:43,309
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

185
00:12:43,721 --> 00:12:45,942
A times x is four, seven.

186
00:12:46,380 --> 00:12:51,593
The stretch gives two, two, and the rotation gives minus two, one.

187
00:12:52,005 --> 00:12:54,160
R S and S R are different.

188
00:12:54,585 --> 00:13:01,846
And X times w scores the houses: two hundred fifty-five, one hundred fifty, and three hundred thirty-five.

189
00:13:03,573 --> 00:13:04,237
In summary.

190
00:13:04,663 --> 00:13:10,381
A matrix is a rectangular grid of numbers, with m rows and n columns.

191
00:13:10,793 --> 00:13:13,546
As a table, each row is a data point.

192
00:13:13,985 --> 00:13:23,692
As a machine, it moves vectors: A times x is a linear combination of the columns, and the columns show where e one and e two land.

193
00:13:24,606 --> 00:13:28,263
Matrix multiplication is one machine after another.

194
00:13:28,675 --> 00:13:34,765
An m by n matrix times an n by p matrix gives m by p, and the order matters.

195
00:13:35,218 --> 00:13:40,058
The transpose swaps rows and columns, and the identity does nothing.

196
00:13:40,470 --> 00:13:46,202
Next, in video three: basis and rank, how many directions are really there.

197
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:47,186
See you there.

198
00:13:49,106 --> 00:13:50,928
This video was made by David R.

199
00:13:51,367 --> 00:13:54,598
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

200
00:13:55,156 --> 00:13:55,874
Thanks for watching.
